1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,060
Hikage-machi asks how open city data can
support research on bus stop shade.

2
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:09,920
This update reports a data-intake
milestone for Takamatsu, Japan.

3
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:14,800
It is an application in development, not a
finished solar exposure tool.

4
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:20,780
The actual city archive contains 268,154

5
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:23,700
building objects with unique identifiers.

6
00:00:24,530 --> 00:00:29,180
2,160 contain level 2 detail elements.

7
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:35,500
A separate Kotoden Bus timetable snapshot
contains 658 stop records.

8
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,660
10 numerical and data consistency checks
passed.

9
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:44,180
Two different counting methods agreed
across 408 building files.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,800
A separate reviewer checked source hashes,

11
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:50,180
timetable relationships, and a sample of
geometry.

12
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:54,680
These checks establish consistency within
their stated scope.

13
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:58,540
They do not establish building accuracy,
sunlight

14
00:00:58,540 --> 00:01:01,400
exposure, cooling effects, or energy
savings.

15
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:06,520
The next research stage needs a reference
shading calculation, dated service

16
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:09,540
patterns, and validation on actual bus
stops.

17
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:12,980
Code, sources, and limitations are
documented.

18
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:16,280
Research by Francisco Angulo de Lafuente
